  <p>Reviewed October 16, 2026. The settings below come from the help center and the maker’s documentation. The prompts are proposed shot plans, not results from a product-video test.</p>

  <h2>What changed from Kling 2.6 and 2.5 Turbo?</h2>

  <p>Kling 3.0 is a video-generation model. Its maker documents broader scene control than the earlier versions, including multi-shot generation and element references. Check the app separately for the controls it exposes.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Maker documentation</th>
          <th scope="col">What it says</th>
          <th scope="col">Consequence for a product brief</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Kling 2.5 Turbo release</th>
          <td>Improved prompt understanding and dynamic camera motion</td>
          <td>Give the motion a clear purpose</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Kling 2.6 guide</th>
          <td>Visuals and native audio generated together</td>
          <td>Specify the sound as part of the scene</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Kling 3.0 guide</th>
          <td>Flexible duration, multi-shot generation, element references</td>
          <td>Separate model capabilities from the inputs your app exposes</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Kling’s VIDEO 3.0 guide documents a duration range of 3 to 15 seconds and native speech in Chinese, English, Japanese, Korean, and Spanish.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Kling AI, February 6, 2026, VIDEO 3.0 model user guide, the maker’s documentation, not a measured product-consistency test.</p>

  <p>Kuaishou’s September 26, 2025 release describes better prompt comprehension and dynamic motion for 2.5 Turbo. Kling’s December 16, 2025 guide describes 2.6’s combined visual and audio generation. Sources: Kuaishou investor relations release on Kling 2.5 Turbo; Kling AI, VIDEO 2.6 audio user guide.</p>

  <p>These are maker claims, not evidence that a particular bottle, shoe, or watch will render correctly.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Native 4K video output was added to the Kling 3.0 series during the second quarter of 2026, according to its maker.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Kuaishou, August 19, 2026 interim-results announcement, a record of a feature release rather than an independent image-quality test.</p>

  <p><a href="/app/engines">Engines in the hubStudio app</a> lists the choices. Confirm that the selected engine takes your source image before writing a brief around that input.</p>

  <h2>What belongs in a product-shot prompt?</h2>

  <p>Start with the product’s visible identity. “A luxury bottle” leaves most of the object unspecified. “A squat amber glass bottle with rounded shoulders, a black screw cap, and a blank cream paper label” gives the shot something concrete to preserve.</p>

  <p>The surface belongs in the prompt too. The bottle might stand on stone, painted wood, or a bathroom shelf. If scale matters, add a familiar object such as a drinking glass. Avoid adding props whose movement would distract from the product.</p>

  <p>Now decide what moves. The product can stay still. Set the light direction. Give the camera a move and an ending. Reserve room in that ending for a headline if the clip needs one.</p>

  <p>This follows the subject, movement, and scene structure in Kling’s Text-to-Video guide, dated November 24, 2025. Its camera and lighting terms remain useful vocabulary; its older duration examples are not the source for the current settings table.</p>

  <p>If exact packaging text matters, do not treat a sentence in a prompt as an approved label file. Keep the intended wording in the brief and compare it with the output. Use blank labels only for these practice shots. They let you judge the movement before attempting real packaging; they cannot replace approved label artwork in a finished product ad.</p>

  <h2>Which camera move should you ask for?</h2>

  <p>Kling’s camera-control guide, dated August 13, 2026, recommends keeping one main move per shot rather than combining several commands. The vocabulary below comes from that guide. The product risks are editorial inspection criteria, not measured failure rates.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Move</th>
          <th scope="col">Prompt wording</th>
          <th scope="col">Intended effect</th>
          <th scope="col">Inspect closely</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Push in</th>
          <td>Slowly move closer to the bottle</td>
          <td>Make a detail larger in frame</td>
          <td>Label edges and cap proportions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Pull back</th>
          <td>Slowly pull back from the shoe</td>
          <td>Reveal more of its surroundings</td>
          <td>Product scale as the view widens</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Pan</th>
          <td>Pan right to bring the stationary product into view</td>
          <td>Shift the view sideways</td>
          <td>Whether the product stays visible</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Tilt</th>
          <td>Tilt upward from the base to the cap</td>
          <td>Reveal the product’s height</td>
          <td>Whether the reveal crops essential detail</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Orbit</th>
          <td>Make a shallow orbit around the object</td>
          <td>Expose part of another side</td>
          <td>Newly revealed surfaces and reflections</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Track</th>
          <td>Track alongside the moving product</td>
          <td>Follow the product while it moves through the scene</td>
          <td>Contact with the surface and direction of travel</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Static</th>
          <td>Keep the camera locked off</td>
          <td>Hold a fixed composition</td>
          <td>Unrequested object movement</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>For a labeled bottle, start frontally. An orbit asks the render to show surfaces that a frontal description may leave vague. On polished metal, inspect the highlight as well as the outline: a bright reflection moving across a bezel should not become a new ridge in the metal.</p>

  <p>Specify whether the camera moves or the product turns. If a shoe must remain still while the camera retreats, write that explicitly rather than asking for a “dynamic reveal.”</p>

